## The bridge from module 3's direct mapping to module 2's vocabulary.
##
## Module 3 exports the property graph mechanically: every label becomes a
## `voc:` class, every property and every edge type a `voc:` predicate. That
## mapping is honest and it is also parochial -- it speaks only our names.
##
## These axioms say, once, how our names relate to the Public Contracts
## Ontology. Nothing in the data changes. What changes is that a reasoner can
## derive the PCO form, and then **the five SHACL shapes written by hand in
## module 2 run on module 3's data without being touched.**
##
## That is the argument for RDF stated as something testable rather than as a
## slogan: constraints written against someone else's vocabulary apply to your
## data, because a mapping layer made the two meet.
##
## Written against what module 2 actually targets, read out of its notebook on
## 5/10/2026: pc:Contract and kgtp:ContractRecord as classes, and the properties
## pc:startDate, pc:actualEndDate, pc:awardDate, pc:contractingAuthority, plus
## kgtp:revisionOf and kgtp:revisionNumber inside its SPARQL constraint.

@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix voc:  <http://kgtp.org/voc/> .
@prefix kgtp: <http://kgtp.org/> .
@prefix pc:   <http://purl.org/procurement/public-contracts#> .
@prefix gr:   <http://purl.org/goodrelations/v1#> .

# --- classes ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Both of ours are contracts in PCO's sense. The second line is the one that
# earns its keep: module 2 had to name kgtp:ContractRecord explicitly in every
# shape, because rdflib materialises nothing and SHACL only follows what the
# data says. Here the subclass axiom becomes data, and a shape targeting
# pc:Contract alone reaches the 185,730 revisions by itself.
voc:Contract        rdfs:subClassOf pc:Contract .
voc:ContractRecord  rdfs:subClassOf pc:Contract , kgtp:ContractRecord .

voc:Organization    rdfs:subClassOf gr:BusinessEntity .
voc:Department      rdfs:subClassOf gr:BusinessEntity .

# --- dates ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Decision 7 keeps a typed date beside the source text; only the typed one is
# bridged, because a shape comparing against xsd:date cannot read "07/30/2001".
voc:startDate     rdfs:subPropertyOf pc:startDate .
voc:endDate       rdfs:subPropertyOf pc:actualEndDate .
voc:approvalDate  rdfs:subPropertyOf pc:awardDate .

# --- parties ----------------------------------------------------------------
# RECORDED_DEPARTMENT and not ASSIGNS_CONTRACT: both say the same thing, but
# PCO's contractingAuthority runs contract -> authority, which is the direction
# the record-level edge already has. Bridging the entity-level edge instead
# would need owl:inverseOf and therefore an OWL ruleset rather than RDFS.
voc:RECORDED_DEPARTMENT rdfs:subPropertyOf pc:contractingAuthority .
voc:HAS_VENDOR          rdfs:subPropertyOf pc:bidder .
voc:RECORDED_VENDOR     rdfs:subPropertyOf pc:bidder .

# --- revisions --------------------------------------------------------------
# What module 2's sh:sparql constraint reads. `revisionNumber` has no
# counterpart in our export: the mechanical mapping carried the record's `id`,
# and the revision number is that id's position within its contract, which is
# not a property any row holds. Declared here so the gap is visible rather than
# silently making the constraint vacuous -- see the note in SHACL.md.
voc:INCLUDED_IN_CONTRACT rdfs:subPropertyOf kgtp:revisionOf .

# --- names ------------------------------------------------------------------
voc:name rdfs:subPropertyOf gr:legalName .
